# Components

Keep screens declarative and thin. Extract logic into hooks; extract UI into primitives.

## Size & responsibility

- Components stay under ~150 lines. Split when they cross.
- One responsibility per component. If you say "and" describing it, split.
- Never place business logic directly in JSX. Compute, then render.
- Use early returns to reduce nesting.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — logic in JSX, deep nesting
return (
  <View>
    {phone.length === 10 && !isOnline ? <OfflineBanner /> : (
      isPending ? <Spinner /> : <Button onPress={() => sendOtp({ ... })} />
    )}
  </View>
);

// ✅ GOOD — derive once, render flat
const showOffline = isPhoneValid && !isOnline;
const handleSubmit = () => sendOtp({ phoneE164: normalizeToE164(phone) });

if (isPending) return <Spinner />;
return (
  <View>
    {showOffline ? <OfflineBanner /> : <Button onPress={handleSubmit} />}
  </View>
);
```

## Composition

- Prefer composition over prop explosion. If a component has > ~6 props, split it
  or accept `children`.
- Build reusable UI primitives early — `Button`, `Input`, `Card`. Screens compose
  primitives, primitives never compose screens.
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks (`use*`).

## Hooks inside components

- Hooks at the top of the function only. No conditional hooks.
- Stable keys in lists — never `index` for dynamic data.
- Avoid inline functions inside large lists — hoist or memoize.
- Avoid side effects during rendering.
